# Manager Surface Rules

Purpose: protect team boundaries, annotation integrity, and manager trust.

## 1. Team visibility boundaries

- Managers see only reps on their own team.
- Bookers never see other bookers' data.
- A rep removed from a team becomes inaccessible to the former manager immediately for future reads.

## 2. Priority queue explainability

- Every rep in the coaching priority queue must show the top 2 reasons they are ranked there.
- Do not show only a raw score without explanation.

## 3. Annotation integrity

- Manager annotations require immutable audit trail entries for edits and deletes.
- Rep-visible coaching notes and manager-only private notes are separate concepts.
- Private notes never surface to reps, never become drills automatically, and never enter manager packets.
- AI observations and manager annotations must be visually and semantically distinct.
- Same-team co-managers (any user with an active `ManagerAssignment` for the rep, or an org owner) CAN see each other's `manager_only` notes on shared reps — Phase K (Manager Audit) relaxed the previous "author only" rule to prevent double-handling. Bookers still never see `manager_only` notes under any circumstance, and cross-tenant isolation is still enforced by `assertCanReadSession`.

## 4. Manager packet fallback

- Weekly manager packet generation must follow tiered fallback:
  - full
  - light
  - minimal
  - skip
- Do not pad thin data into a fake full packet.

## 5. Rollout caution

- Beta metrics visible to managers must be labeled experimental.
- Manager-visible beta metrics do not become booker-facing truth until promoted to released.

## 6. Manager notifications (Phase H)

- Allowed notification types live in `MANAGER_NOTIFICATION_TYPES` in shared taxonomy. Do not emit strings outside that enum.
- Emission is debounced by `MANAGER_NOTIFICATION_DEBOUNCE_HOURS` per `(managerUserId, type, subjectKey)`. Do not add a second emitter path that bypasses the debouncer.
- Notification payloads (`data`, `message`, `title`) must not contain transcript content, call audio, prospect words, or rep DM content. Rep names, numeric deltas, session ids, and normalized objection labels only.
- `deepLinkPath` is server-authored. Frontend must not rewrite paths client-side — changing a route means updating the emitter.
- Bell visibility follows manager-dashboard access. Bookers never see the bell and never receive notifications addressed to a manager.
- A notification subsystem failure must never crash queue recompute or the packet-sent route. Log and continue.

## 7. Coaching-line sensitivity (Phase O)

- The effective sensitivity for a rep MUST be resolved via `resolveRepSensitivity` (backend) or `resolveEffectiveSensitivity` (shared pure helper). Do not consult `Organization.coachingLineSensitivity` directly on a new call site — it ignores per-rep overrides.
- Resolution rule is strictest-wins: any active `ManagerAssignment.sensitivityOverride = 'strict'` overrides a `'recommended'` override from another manager on the same rep. Missing org membership fails closed to `'strict'`.
- Setting or clearing a per-rep override MUST write an `AuditEvent` (`coaching.sensitivity_override.set` / `.cleared`) with the effective sensitivity after the change.
- Every action on the manager approval queue (approve/edit/reject a suggested line) MUST write an `AuditEvent` (`coaching.suggested_line.{action}`) with the resolved sensitivity recorded in metadata — that is the provenance chain reviewers rely on.
- Booker-visible call reviews: when a coaching line exists but is held for review, render the neutral "waiting for manager approval" chip. Never display the raw AI line text to a booker under `strict` sensitivity. Under `recommended`, a suggested/generated example may be rep-visible only when the shared line policy marks it as a grounded spoken example with no gate reasons; Prompt A, footer parsing, persisted provenance, DTO projection, and frontend visibility must all carry that same effective sensitivity.
- The `approval_queue_stale` reminder fires only when BOTH `APPROVAL_QUEUE_STALE_AGE_HOURS` (age of oldest pending) and `APPROVAL_QUEUE_STALE_MIN_PENDING` (backlog size) thresholds are crossed. Reminder cadence is weekly, overriding the default 4h debounce. Thresholds live in shared config — do not inline literals.

## 8. Multi-manager coordination (Phase K)

- `PriorityQueueEntryDTO.fellowManagers` lists ONLY co-managers actively assigned to the same rep (active `ManagerAssignment` rows), and never includes the caller themselves. Owners see co-managers too so they can spot who else is engaging a rep.
- The separate `DrillEscalationClaim` lifecycle was retired in DISP-006: it had no reachable writer or visible product surface. Do not restore claim/release routes, TTLs, DTOs, CSV columns, or rep-facing claim copy without a new approved product decision.

## 9. Export privacy (Phase R)

- Manager exports (`coachingApi.exportTeamObjectionsCsv` and the API-only priority projection) must never carry transcripts, raw audio, prospect quotes, `manager_only` annotation content, or unapproved AI suggested lines. Only fields already safe-rendered to the manager UI may enter the export.
- Free-text fields that MIGHT contain customer PII (currently only `approvedReplacementLine`) MUST be piped through `redactSensitiveText` before serialization. The in-product view still renders the unredacted text to authorized managers; the CSV is defense-in-depth because files are forwarded, attached to emails, and live outside the product boundary.
- Rep names and manager names are internal identifiers, not customer PII, and remain in exports. Session IDs are opaque and safe to export.
- Every export must write an `AuditEvent` (`coaching.export.priority_queue` / `coaching.export.team_objections`) with the acting manager, team size, and row count. This is the revocation / forensic trail if a CSV leaks.
- Adding a new column to any manager export is a privacy review: confirm the field cannot contain customer quotes, audio, or prospect-side content before shipping.

## 10. Mobile responsiveness (Phase L)

- Manager surfaces (Dashboard Overview, TeamInsightsPage, ManagerPacketPage, ManagerNotificationBell) must render without horizontal page scroll at 360px viewport width. Wide artifacts (Rep × Objection matrix) are the exception and must be wrapped in a single `overflow-x-auto` container so the rest of the page stays in-flow.
- Breakpoint pattern: 1-col mobile, 2-col tablet (`sm:`), full grid desktop (`lg:` / `xl:`). Do not introduce large multi-column layouts without a single-column mobile fallback.
- Touch targets on manager actions (approval queue buttons and notification dropdown items) must clear a ~24px minimum. No `text-[10px]` + `px-2 py-0.5` action buttons.
- Floating / absolute panels (notification dropdown, tooltips) must cap at `max-w-[90vw]` and `max-h-[60vh]` (or smaller) so they never exceed the mobile viewport.
- Priority queue rows, packet cards, and header action rows must use `flex-wrap` — never a single-row `flex` that can push critical content offscreen.
- Export CSV buttons (Phase J) are desktop-optional affordances. They must remain visible on mobile but may collapse into the wrap flow rather than occupying their own row.

## 11. Cross-persona bridges (Phase P)

- `ImprovementPlanDTO.escalationCoach` and its claim-derived bridge are retired with `DrillEscalationClaim`. Booker coaching remains grounded in the improvement-plan and call-review authorities; no manager-identity claim chip is produced.
